# Machine-readable citation metadata (Citation File Format 1.2.0).
# GitHub reads this file to render the "Cite this repository" button, and
# cffconvert validates it on every push (.github/workflows/ci.yml) -- a CFF
# file with one wrong field renders NOTHING and fails silently, which is the
# defect class this repository exists to be careful about.
#
# BEFORE RELEASE, the owner should add:
#   - person authors under `authors:` if the artifact should cite individuals
#     rather than the organisation (family-names / given-names / orcid),
#   - `repository-code:` once there is a public URL,
#   - a DOI under `identifiers:` if the Zenodo path in CONTRIBUTING.md is taken.
# None of those is invented here.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this benchmark, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  Can an agent implement a method from its paper? A pre-registered benchmark
  of retrieval conditions for code-implementation agents
abstract: >-
  A pre-registered benchmark that asks whether a coding agent, given a paper's
  method name, entry point and one sentence of description, can produce a
  working implementation -- varying only what retrieval the agent is given
  across four arms (no tools, web search, a knowledge graph, and a flat index
  over the same code with the graph removed). Four runs, each against a
  pre-registration committed before any subject token was spent, 75.88 USD of
  total subject spend. The headline result is a null about the authors' own
  product: a flat BM25 + cosine index over 118,400 code samples matched the
  graph arm on every task (0 discordant pairs in 24, sign test p = 1.00), and
  with the answer held out neither arm beat having no tools at all (24/48 vs
  24/48 vs 23/48, 95% CI on the graph-vs-flat difference [-11.5%, +15.6%]).
  Every published number is re-derivable from the artifacts in the repository.
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Syntology"
    website: "https://syntology.ai"
version: "1.6"
date-released: "2026-09-13"
license: Apache-2.0
url: "https://syntology.ai"
keywords:
